WEBVTT

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:05.125
Manipulation behaviors vary widely across objects and scenes,

00:00:05.175 --> 00:00:08.350
yet they share a small set of reusable skills.

00:00:08.650 --> 00:00:12.875
Planning with these skills helps agents generalize to new tasks.

00:00:13.175 --> 00:00:16.525
But an agent can only plan with the skills it knows.

00:00:17.225 --> 00:00:23.025
Recovering skills from experience, the inverse of planning, builds this knowledge over time,

00:00:23.125 --> 00:00:26.475
and yields skill data for training future agents.

00:00:27.075 --> 00:00:30.675
Vision-language models describe single events well.

00:00:30.975 --> 00:00:35.150
But can they organize a stream of events into reusable skills?

00:00:37.150 --> 00:00:44.900
We call this Streaming Embodied Skill Discovery: a model watches videos in sequence, and keeps a persistent skill library,

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:48.625
which shapes its later decisions: reuse a known skill,

00:00:50.355 --> 00:00:52.205
or create a new one.

00:00:53.205 --> 00:01:00.380
Video2Skill covers robot tabletop manipulation and human kitchen activity, and tests three abilities:

00:01:00.380 --> 00:01:06.705
locating events in time, grouping the same transformation, and deciding when to reuse or create.

00:01:07.305 --> 00:01:14.130
Across 19 open-source VLMs, many group events near chance, and scale does not consistently help.

00:01:14.730 --> 00:01:19.355
Their errors depend on how perception and library updates are coupled:

00:01:19.355 --> 00:01:25.955
joint models merge distinct transformations, while models updating from text duplicate recurring ones.

00:01:26.655 --> 00:01:32.655
Fine-tuning, including our CLaRe method, improves grouping, but exposes a deeper bottleneck.

00:01:32.755 --> 00:01:40.405
Trained models consolidate familiar skills, yet rarely expand the library, stalling below half the reference size.

00:01:40.805 --> 00:01:46.880
Transformations unseen in training are located in time, but almost never given a new skill.

00:01:47.580 --> 00:01:52.580
Recognizing when existing skills are insufficient is the central challenge.
